There’s a coherent architecture underneath all of that.

Faith, in the way you’re using it, functions as a scale-restoring mechanism. When immediate pain, fear, humiliation, uncertainty, or scarcity compresses the horizon, trust in something larger—Jesus, God, a mission, a family, a future self—can reopen the frame.

Pain tends to say:

\text{this moment} = \text{everything}

A larger perspective answers:

\text{this moment} \subset \text{a much larger trajectory}

That doesn’t make pain unreal. It changes its jurisdiction.

And your short-term/long-term point matters. Neither should dominate absolutely. Pure short-term optimization becomes impulsivity; pure long-term optimization can sacrifice the only life actually being lived. Better is multiscale control:

\text{act locally while steering globally}

You eat today and plan for retirement. Protect yourself now and preserve relationships that matter later. Endure discomfort when it serves something larger, but don’t worship suffering merely because you can withstand it.

That last distinction is important with the warrior metaphor. Pain can be information, training stress, initiation, warning, or unavoidable cost. But mastery isn’t becoming incapable of hearing pain. It is being able to determine:

Is this pain asking me to continue, adapt, recover, leave, or seek help?

Otherwise the tool begins wielding the warrior.

The human-versus-nature framing can undergo the same transition you described:

\text{nature as adversary}
\rightarrow
\text{nature as system we inhabit}

Humans survived partly by becoming extraordinarily good at cooperation, prediction, toolmaking, cultural transmission, and environmental modification—not simply by becoming better predators. Eventually our power became large enough that “defeat nature” stopped making much sense, because degrading the surrounding system degrades ourselves.

So:

us vs. nature → us within nature → us shaping nature → responsibility for what we shape.

Likewise, hunter and prey are useful cognitive positions rather than permanent identities. Prey-attention asks, Where is the threat? Hunter-attention asks, Where is the opportunity? Builder-attention asks, What can exist here that does not exist yet?

That third state may actually be the important evolutionary transition.

Once survival pressure loosens, the question changes from:

“How do we avoid being eaten?”

to:

“What kind of world do creatures with this much agency choose to make?”

And “us and them” is a better answer than permanent conquest. Mutualism frequently beats domination over sufficiently long horizons.

Your stick-and-stone line captures something worth keeping too: tools amplify human faculties, but they are not the source of those faculties. Strip away a particular device and humans can rebuild because the deeper inheritance is learning, coordination, imagination, memory, hands, language, and persistence.

The equipment changes.

The generative capacity remains.